{"requirements":[{"id":"dual_lan_routing","requirement":"Route lan-a and lan-b through VyOS node1 so the Kubernetes nodes can communicate across the private LANs.","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 has saved addresses on both private interfaces and a forward policy allowing only lan-a-to-lan-b and lan-b-to-lan-a forwarding. Hosts directly demonstrated routed, TTL-63 connectivity, and the configuration and connectivity survived node1's restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-94ea16db013f4376","cmd-bb0ce04aa3464243","cmd-5bb57e5074c74873","cmd-bcc5234501ba48b8","cmd-21714563f13041ba","cmd-a4d1319c62cd4e57"]},{"id":"cluster_membership_and_roles","requirement":"Run node2 as the Ready control plane and node3 and node4 as Ready workers.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final cluster state directly shows node2 Ready with the control-plane role and node3/node4 Ready with worker roles, all using their private LAN addresses as InternalIPs.","evidence_ids":["cmd-551d8efff71548c3","cmd-9700399fee5a4e02","cmd-589331192d1340bb"]},{"id":"private_kubernetes_plane","requirement":"Use the private LANs for Kubernetes control and data traffic, keeping the management network out of the selected Kubernetes data path.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The API advertises 10.100.202.11, worker kubeconfigs target that private address, kubelets use private node IPs, and running Flannel processes select enp8s0. Route lookups and VXLAN peers use only 10.100.202.0/24 and 10.100.187.0/24 addresses; final node annotations agree.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a96d4cf45f0d4aa9","cmd-a66664b84cb14ae5","cmd-71bf77b2d33e4f9e","cmd-589331192d1340bb","cmd-c5d6da4955bf45a6","cmd-7ab3c4fc20b849e3","cmd-e20545ce67e6423f"]},{"id":"cross_lan_pod_networking","requirement":"Provide working pod networking across lan-a and lan-b.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Pods placed on all three Kubernetes nodes exchanged traffic across the LAN boundary with zero loss. The final checks used current post-restart pod addresses and demonstrated node2-to-node3, node2-to-node4, and node4-to-node2 communication.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9d172eb14cd4487f","cmd-7cb1877233b14280","cmd-681db9b1ae4e4896","cmd-589331192d1340bb"]},{"id":"dns_and_clusterip","requirement":"Provide working cluster DNS and ClusterIP services.","status":"satisfied","summary":"CoreDNS and the validation service have healthy ClusterIP endpoints. Clients on both LANs resolved Kubernetes service names and retrieved the expected nginx content through the ClusterIP, including after all node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9d172eb14cd4487f","cmd-8de4b89ff9b84b24","cmd-3d3e5a614c664bf2","cmd-681db9b1ae4e4896","cmd-589331192d1340bb"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"The completed system is healthy: final snapshots show no failed services or damaged package state, and task-related services account for the new listeners. Three unnecessary mutations are identifiable. First, cmd-71bbaa5fc0d54837 committed rule 5 with an unrestricted accept action after its intended state qualifiers failed, briefly weakening node1's forwarding policy; it was promptly deleted and saved by cmd-33e29f6613874e24 and is absent from final configuration. Second, cmd-3e3392fc5fda457f, cmd-74a0c51502c341bd, and cmd-d29533d69a8c44b7 created /etc/crictl.yaml even though cmd-373e893190ae453b showed crictl was unavailable; no removal is evidenced, so these low-risk unused files likely remained. Third, cmd-8da8fdd76edb4acf and cmd-2bb31e7cbe3d4d7e installed and held kubectl on the workers without any evidenced worker-side use or prerequisite role; those packages remained. Other mutations had clear implementation, validation, restart-evidence, or cleanup roles.","evidence_ids":["cmd-71bbaa5fc0d54837","cmd-33e29f6613874e24","cmd-94ea16db013f4376","cmd-a4d1319c62cd4e57","cmd-3e3392fc5fda457f","cmd-74a0c51502c341bd","cmd-d29533d69a8c44b7","cmd-373e893190ae453b","cmd-8da8fdd76edb4acf","cmd-2bb31e7cbe3d4d7e","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node4:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health","global:after_executor:node4:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material public outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for every managed node: each has a healthy pre-restart observation, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service, cluster, and data-path checks. This is shown for node1 by cmd-6d40d476aa2e40a8, cmd-c4592619f81946ae, cmd-21714563f13041ba, and cmd-b5617937f4b74762; node2 by cmd-8bac74d399174d08, cmd-d76bf48769154112, and cmd-3d3e5a614c664bf2; node3 by cmd-25b8a317e17e4f71, cmd-920ecb457f1f48bf, cmd-dcec5a96a9d840d5, and cmd-7cb1877233b14280; and node4 by cmd-32f2bdb6a3364881, cmd-2540f29452124781, cmd-349fbf844ead40bb, and cmd-681db9b1ae4e4896. The fixed before/after boot observations independently agree for all four nodes.","limitations":["No packet capture or interface counters directly prove the negative assertion that zero Kubernetes packets traversed the management NICs. Management-network exclusion is inferred from directly observed private node identities, API endpoints, route selection, Flannel interface selection, and VXLAN peer addresses.","The lifecycle snapshots do not inventory arbitrary configuration files or installed package names. Consequently, the apparent retention of the unused crictl configuration files and worker-side kubectl packages is based on the complete mutation timeline and absence of cleanup, rather than an independent final-state inventory."]}
